1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Even this late in the winter, even in March, it's been cold.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
It's been so cold- if we had a longer thermometer we would have frozen to death.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
If it's been so cold, we were out camping, boiled a pot of water on the fire,

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
put it aside. it froze so fast. it was still a little warm.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
It was so cold that when you would speak your words would freeze in mid-air

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
and you'd have to throw them in a frying pan to have a conversation.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
That's the way it goes, folks.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
This is podcast number 367..

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast. This is Clark Green.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
We're arriving at the end of our fundamental series and I have appreciated your feedback along the way.

12
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Our discussion today turned out to be, well, not terribly long, but fairly long,

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
And I think we should just jump right into it. So let's get started, shall we?

14
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:14,000
This discussion today of engaging scouts in leadership

15
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,000
is, as they say, the penultimate installment of our foundation series,

16
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
And I'm going to wrap that up next time around with a discussion of constructive discipline.

17
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Now, to hear most scouts talk about it, a youth-led troop is kind of the brass ring of scouting,

18
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:42,000
And as much as this is talked about and sought after. I have to ask exactly the same questions that we've been asking for the past couple of podcasts:

19
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Why is youth leadership even there? And then, why is it important? How do we get there?

20
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
And both of those questions are reasonably easy to answer. Why should scouting be youth-led?

21
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Why should youth engage in leadership? Why not just have adults do the whole thing?

22
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Why not just prepare and present a program of activities to them?

23
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
If we're accepting the premise that scouting is a game, part of playing the game is actually playing the game.

24
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Otherwise, all the coaches would be out on the court during the basketball game just kind of pointing the way for a player to go right,

25
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Say: okay, will you dribble over here, pass to him. It would take away the teamwork aspect of the game.

26
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
It would take away the strategic aspect of the game and the thinking aspect of it.

27
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
As for how this is supposed to function, that's what we're going to talk about here.

28
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
But basically it functions by scouts actually just leading. It's that simple

29
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:45,000
And in my experience there is a lot of misunderstanding and confusion about this particular subject

30
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
and nearly any other aspect of scouting. I think a lot of times it's mishandled,

31
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:56,000
It gets misinterpreted and misunderstood, But you'll be happy to know that it's actually very, very simple

32
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
And if you've been following this series, you won't be surprised when I say engaging youth in leadership

33
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
is really really simple, direct and uncomplicated.

34
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
But like most easy, simple things, it's also endlessly complex, in a way, when you start to work with it.

35
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Somewhere along in this series, I've definitely alluded to the idea that scouting is kind of like playing golf

36
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
or fly fishing or something like that. It's something you can learn how to do in a couple of hours

37
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,000
and then spend the rest of your life perfecting your technique.

38
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
I'm going to include a few techniques here, but basically we're going to be talking in big ideas today.

39
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
In connection with all of the other things that we've been saying in this series,

40
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
let me begin by saying this: your goal as a scouter is not building leadership skills or creating leaders.

41
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Our main aim, our main goal, is what It's: developing character by applying the patrol method.

42
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
If we focus on building leaders and leadership skills, we're going to miss the point.

43
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
If we focus on building character, we're going to build leaders. You can't help it.

44
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,000
So if you think the main point of scouting is training and building a certain kind of leader.

45
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:13,000
you need to stop and refocus. You're approaching this from a skewed perspective,

46
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
So follow my lead for a moment. Look at the simple promise in the Scout Oath to help other people at all times,

47
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
because that's where anything like leadership begins: helping other people at all times.

48
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
If you think about it- the Scout Oath and Law- that's as close as it gets to saying anything about leadership at all.

49
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
The Scout Oath describes our commitment to others in this way,

50
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
and the Scout Law gives us a set of ideals to strive towards,

51
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:52,000
and these are the things that constitute a person of character who is able to provide service by leading others.

52
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:59,000
And once you start to see the oath and the law as a definition of leadership,

53
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
things begin to open up, because to be a Scout is to be a leader.

54
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Every Scout is a leader and they lead all the time.

55
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Now, if that sounds impossible or improbable to you, you have a certain view or definition of leadership

56
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
that I'm going to help you try to change.

57
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
If we limit our definition of leadership to being in charge and directing others,

58
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:33,000
we'll miss 75% of what leadership actually is, because there are really four kinds of leadership.

59
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
The first one is that you lead yourself, You look after yourself.

60
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:42,000
You make sure that you're prepared, rested and ready to do whatever it is you're going to be doing.

61
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
The second kind of leadership is really cooperative following.

62
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,000
You're supporting the leadership of others by cooperatively following along, being a part of a team.

63
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
The third kind of leadership is helping others, because if you don't do the first two kinds,

64
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
you're not going to be able to help anybody.

65
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
And the fourth kind of leadership, and the one that we get kind of enthralled with,

66
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
is that kind of directive leadership, decision-making and directing.

67
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Now, some situations demand a specific kind of leadership, yet all four ways are equal in importance

68
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:20,000
And in many situations we're all four kinds of leader at once.

69
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
So if you want to help your scouts build the skills of leadership,

70
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:32,000
we have to start with the attitudes and perspectives that inspire us towards that.

71
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
by helping other people, the promises in the oath, the ideals of the law,

72
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:44,000
create a strong sense of duty, responsibility and resolve aimed at serving others,

73
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,000
which is the ultimate definition of what a leader is.

74
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,000
So when you go and you look at your scouts, being a leader is not something

75
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
that they will become someday in the future.

76
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
They are doing that right now

77
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
And this is like the key.

78
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
This is the key to changing your perspective.

79
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,000
If you don't believe me: observe, watch them and start to identify

80
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:15,000
the ways that they are employing one of those four kinds of leadership.

81
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:23,000
So leadership is not something granted to certain older scouts, or power and privilege.

82
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
It's about service.

83
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Scout leadership is not defined by organizational job descriptions and patches and charts.

84
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Scout leadership is not a structure of power and privilege.

85
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:41,000
If you are a scout, you simply can't avoid learning the lessons of leadership all the time.

86
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:49,000
So the first big step for us as scouts is kind of dismissing our idea of

87
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:56,000
what we think leadership ought to be and recognizing all of these leadership qualities in our scouts

88
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
and see them as they develop.

89
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:04,000
So let's focus on engaging our scouts in directive leadership.

90
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:10,000
How do we support them leading their troop or their den or their crew?

91
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:15,000
Now, if you're thinking in terms of a cadre of scouts sitting around a paper-filled table,

92
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
you know, discussing options and making plans, then that's a very limited perspective.

93
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Put that particular vision out of your head.

94
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:30,000
We'd like to think that what our scouts do and how they do it defines being youth led.

95
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
but it doesn't.

96
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Young people lead themselves all the time.

97
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:39,000
It comes very naturally to them and you might not see it if you don't look for it, but it's there.

98
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:46,000
What you are going to do as a scouter is recognize that they are already practicing leadership

99
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:52,000
and build on it. so scouts have the satisfaction and character-building experience of leading their own troop.

100
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,000
So engaging scouts in leadership is really very simple.

101
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
You let them lead.

102
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Developing leadership skill is equally simple.

103
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,000
You let them lead.

104
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,000
And when you let them lead, they begin to develop some skill.

105
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Training leaders is not the same as developing leaders.

106
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Everybody ought to stop training leaders and start developing that.

107
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
I can explain what I mean by that by going back to the game analogy.

108
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
How did you learn to shoot a basketball, if you ever did?

109
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Well, you can learn some things by watching somebody else shoot a basketball,

110
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:34,000
but you don't begin developing the skill until you stand in front of the hoop and throw the ball.

111
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Training is one kind of thing-

112
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
And I'm not saying that training is totally useless.

113
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:44,000
It just has a very limited benefits, because training is mostly sitting and listening.

114
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
Developing is doing,

115
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
And developing is a heck of a lot more fun because you're doing something.

116
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
So I'm going to see if I can avoid that.

117
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
So I'm going to go back to the game analogy.

118
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
I'm going to go back to the game analogy.

119
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
I'm doing something,

120
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:09,000
So I'm going to see if I can avoid climbing up on the soapbox at this particular moment.

121
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
But let me say this:

122
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:18,000
In scouting we're just enthralled with leadership training because it is only partially effective.

123
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,000
We train leaders in scouting using methods adapted from corporate training,

124
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
from military training, from academic kind of training,

125
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
And these methods are boring.

126
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
They're limited in scope.

127
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,000
They're boring.

128
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Did I already say boring?

129
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Yeah,

130
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
They are incredibly boring.

131
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
They're not active and engaged.

132
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:44,000
These methods tend to front load the whole process with lots of information,

133
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
lots of papers, rules, policies, procedures

134
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
and scouts developing leadership don't need information.

135
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
What they need is they need experience.

136
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
They need to have the ball in their hands and start shooting at the hoop.

137
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
So we show them how to shoot, we watch them shoot,

138
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
we ask them questions about things, and then we watch some more,

139
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,000
And they will begin to gain the skill by repetition, and repetition is experienced.

140
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:16,000
So development developing is an incremental process with a lot of small steps along the way.

141
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:23,000
What we need to do is learn to recognize each step and respond to it by encouraging the next step,

142
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:28,000
And one way to begin doing that is, by taking your binder full of helpful forms and outlines

143
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and job descriptions and definitions and all the other trappings of the corporate world

144
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:38,000
and the military world and the academic world and putting them on the shelf

145
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
and start watching your scouts lead,

146
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Because no matter what's going on, no matter where you are,

147
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:49,000
no matter what your scouts are used to, they are leading.

148
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:54,000
You need to find it and then begin incrementally building on that leadership

149
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:00,000
that is already there and you will help them develop and direct their skills in a useful manner.

150
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:05,000
And the way that you do that is letting them lead, and they can't lead if you're leading,

151
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:09,000
so you kind of have to step away from the picture and let them go at it.

152
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
You're thinking about that and you're going:

153
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
okay, that will just be total, utter chaos because I can't.

154
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
I just don't think I can let them do that.

155
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Well, of course it's going to be sort of total, utter chaos,

156
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
but it's not going to be that bad because you're going to be stepping in to fix things

157
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,000
when the whole train is in danger of going off the rails.

158
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:35,000
Your role in developing leadership is responding to the skill level of your scouts.

159
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
in the same way a coach developing a game related skill

160
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,000
develops skill in their players by responding to their skill level.

161
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Your role in developing leadership is responsive.

162
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:52,000
You see nascent skills and you see scouts practicing leadership

163
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
and you respond to wherever they are.

164
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Now that means you may be directing and leading things

165
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:04,000
as they begin to learn more what that's all about, and that's absolutely fine,

166
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:10,000
because you may have to take some time modeling the skill that you want to see developed.

167
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,000
You may end up leading some troop and patrol meetings or some parts of those meetings-

168
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Perfectly acceptable thing to do, so long as it is a means to an end

169
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
and not just standard operating procedure.

170
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
So how do you do that?

171
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:29,000
There are two concepts that I think will help and it's a balancing act,

172
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
and this is balancing direction versus coercion

173
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,000
and shared responsibility versus autonomy.

174
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Let's talk about direction versus coercion.

175
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Scouts are largely directed by the game itself.

176
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
The game has rules,

177
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
A game has policies and procedures

178
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:50,000
and scouts are largely directed by that.

179
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:54,000
You help them understand the boundaries and rules of the game.

180
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
They're not starting with a blank slate,

181
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
They have a field of play.

182
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:02,000
So that's direction, and don't be afraid to direct

183
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:06,000
your scouts, because that's not being overly coercive,

184
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:11,000
That's not interfering with them or anything, as long as it's not standard operating procedure.

185
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,000
They need some direction from you as they learn these skills

186
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and that scale begins to shift as they get more adept at it.

187
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:25,000
In an ideal situation, you direct less and less as they direct more.

188
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
So this is a process.

189
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:31,000
This is not throwing a switch and going from being adult led to being youth led.

190
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
No, this is a process.

191
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
And how long will that process take?

192
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:36,000
I have no idea.

193
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
I don't know your scouts and I don't know you,

194
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
But it takes a while.

195
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
And while we're talking about direction and coercion,

196
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:46,000
we should understand that being youth led does not mean

197
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
that the youth make all the decisions and define everything.

198
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
I mean, every once in a while, I'll hear something like this:

199
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
We don't have patrols because the scouts voted on it.

200
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
They decided they didn't want them and we're youth led.

201
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
So you know, like what are we going to do?

202
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:01,000
They voted on it,

203
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
We don't particularly like it,

204
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,000
But oh well. well, the thing you're going to do is be an adult,

205
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:08,000
Okay,

206
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:12,000
And you're going to direct them and say, no, you can't decide that,

207
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:13,000
I'm afraid

208
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
You know. I mean, imagine a basketball game where the players

209
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
just start carrying the ball rather than dribbling the ball.

210
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
And you ask: you know their coach? you say, hey, wait a minute,

211
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Why are they carrying the ball?

212
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:29,000
And the coach tells you: well, you know, we're a youth led

213
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
organization and the players all decided they'd rather play this

214
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:33,000
way.

215
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:34,000
That doesn't work.

216
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,000
That's not the game.

217
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
Just like any other game, scouting has limitations and definitions.

218
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:42,000
We all play the game within those definitions and limitations.

219
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
The players don't reinvent the game.

220
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:49,000
So we're talking about direction and coercion, and coercion is kind

221
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
of a form of direction, but it's not the form you want to

222
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:53,000
employ.

223
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
Coercion is trying to convince somebody else to make a decision,

224
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
the way that you want them to make the decision. for our

225
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:00,000
purposes here,

226
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Let's use that as a definition.

227
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,000
Well, don't do that.

228
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:06,000
Don't pity pad around trying to convince scouts that this is a

229
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
good idea or that's a good idea,

230
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:11,000
And because you don't want to interfere with their autonomy,

231
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,000
you're powerless to do anything about making them make that

232
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
particular decision,

233
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:19,000
But you're going to coerce them, which is even worse.

234
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
I think if you need something to happen, if you are the

235
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
adult with the greater amount of experience and foresight,

236
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
then your scouts and you believe that a certain course of

237
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
action should be followed. don't pity pad around about trying

238
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
it to convince scouts direct them.

239
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
You can explain your directions.

240
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,000
They may like it,

241
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
They may not, but you can explain in a way that they

242
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:45,000
will understand it in the context of the overall

243
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:48,000
definitions and limitations of the game.

244
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:51,000
And once they understand the reason for that direction,

245
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:55,000
they'll build it into their own experience and use it as a

246
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
way to direct themselves in the future.

247
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Like I said, engaging youth and leadership is not like

248
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
throwing a switch and turning on autopilot and then

249
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:04,000
taking a nap.

250
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,000
It's more like maybe cruise control right,

251
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,000
Turn on cruise control but you don't leave the driver

252
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,000
seat.

253
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,000
You keep your hands on the wheel and you steer and

254
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
you're aware of what's going on around you and you

255
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
might even need to hit the brake now and again.

256
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
You don't give all of your autonomy and decision-making

257
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:23,000
power to the car.

258
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
I hear from scouts who tell me they hope their youth

259
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
leaders will decide this or that or they aren't happy

260
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
with the activities they decided to do, but they don't

261
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
want to interfere or take away the scouts' autonomy

262
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
and they ask me what to do next.

263
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
And a lot of times they try to coerce a certain

264
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
decision and they're kind of disappointed that they

265
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,000
didn't succeed.

266
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
And when they ask me what to do in those situations,

267
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
I tell them. I said you know, direct your scouts to

268
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
do things you want them to do.

269
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:51,000
You are an adult,

270
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
You are in the situation for a reason,

271
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
And if you want them to do a certain thing in a

272
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
certain way, tell them that.

273
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
And that brings me to the idea of shared

274
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
responsibility versus autonomy.

275
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:04,000
For some reason, we have the idea that only truly

276
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
youth-led troop is one where the scouts have

277
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:09,000
complete autonomy and make all the decisions,

278
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:12,000
And there may be times when scouts can make all

279
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
the decisions about a given thing, but it's

280
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
definitely not all the time.

281
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
You can't sit them down with a blank sheet of paper

282
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
and tell them to make a plan.

283
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
Your responsibility as a scouter is defining

284
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:25,000
the expectations and the parameters of the

285
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
plan that they will create,

286
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,000
And this defines what autonomous decisions

287
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
then they are able to make.

288
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
Now, inevitably, because of their laugh of

289
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,000
experience, they will make some poor decisions

290
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
that cause a little trouble,

291
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,000
And you can let these poor decisions that cause

292
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
a little trouble play out as a learning

293
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
experience. as long as you're not playing

294
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
gotcha- and I told you so-

295
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
It's a valuable process, but it's only

296
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
valuable if the trouble created has commenced

297
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
for it with the lesson learned.

298
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:57,000
What I mean by that is: you don't want to

299
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
have an expectation where the scouts are

300
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,000
so autonomous they can decide anything

301
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
and get themselves into real trouble.

302
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
You want their autonomy to be defined by:

303
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
A- the rules of the game, our policies

304
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:12,000
and procedures, and B- the safety of

305
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
themselves and their fellow scouts.

306
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
I mean, every so often I hear a story

307
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
that is something like this: right,

308
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
We were off on a hike and we reached a

309
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
turn in the trail and we allowed

310
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:27,000
the scouts to figure out which

311
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
way to go and they made the wrong turn.

312
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
And two miles later they got to the next

313
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
turn and they figured out they were in

314
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
the wrong place. so we had to turn around

315
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
and go back and we got into camp kind of

316
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
late and everybody was miserable. but it

317
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
was an important lesson for them, right?

318
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:45,000
Well, no, it was just kind of miserable

319
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
because that lesson could have been

320
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
learned a lot quicker and without such drama

321
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
if you directed the scouts a few feet

322
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
past the wrong turn that they made

323
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
and say: okay, you made a wrong turn,

324
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
let's look at the map again

325
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
and let's find out why

326
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
I mean. remember, you are an adult

327
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
and your scouts are not.

328
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
They do not have the perspective

329
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
and experience to make every decision.

330
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
well, nor should they be required

331
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
to make every decision, because we have

332
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
shared responsibility.

333
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
Your responsibility is not to

334
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
expose your scouts to

335
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:20,000
undue drama or risk

336
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
or improper things.

337
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
Their responsibility is to learn

338
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
and develop the skills of leadership.

339
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
That's a shared responsibility.

340
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
We're all in this together.

341
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
We have shared responsibility.

342
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
Another frequent complaint I get from

343
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
scouts is: I just can't get

344
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
the youth leaders to plan.

345
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
they kind of sit there and look at me

346
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
like a dog with a new pan.

347
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
they don't come up with any ideas.

348
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
they don't want to lead.

349
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
I have the same answer for you.

350
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
You're the adult. share the responsibility

351
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
of planning commensurate with the skill

352
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
and experience of your scouts.

353
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
Most often you're going step by step

354
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
and every once in a while

355
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
there's a giant leap, but that's not

356
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
always happening.

357
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Step by step. well,

358
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
you guys need to come up with a plan,

359
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
but what in the world does that mean?

360
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
Scouts are not very good at that

361
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
because they don't have a vast amount

362
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
of experience and they don't

363
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
understand the implications

364
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
of their choices.

365
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
You are the adult. you have more

366
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
experience and have a better understanding

367
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
of the implications of the choices

368
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
that are going to be made.

369
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
So lend them some direction.

370
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
So there's another aspect of this:

371
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
with direction, coercion,

372
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
shared responsibility and autonomy.

373
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
I can illustrate this by using

374
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
the basketball analogy for a moment.

375
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
If I put a bunch of people who've never played basketball

376
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
and never even

377
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
saw the game on a basketball court

378
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
and I explain the rules to them

379
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
and I tell them to play the game, they're going to make

380
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
plenty of mistakes. Now it would be

381
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
foolish for me to get angry or upset

382
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
or frustrated with them,

383
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
because they are learning the game.

384
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
They have no basis of skill

385
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
to go on. they have no basis

386
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
of experience to go on. they just

387
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
know what I've told them. they're doing their best

388
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
and I need to help them

389
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
develop those skills.

390
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
Imagine if I did get angry

391
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
or upset with them and my frustration grew

392
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
over time At some point.

393
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
I would not like these people very much.

394
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
and this same thing happens

395
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
when, okay,

396
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
let's say, you send your scouts off

397
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
to some training event. they come back, you

398
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
sit them around the table and tell them to make plans

399
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
and lead, and they only have the slightest

400
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
idea of what you're talking about and they're all going to

401
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
make a lot of mistakes. You go

402
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
frustrated with this because they're not

403
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
learning quite as quick as you hope

404
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
they would, or you just assume they didn't

405
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
pay attention at the training event

406
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
and you grow frustrated and

407
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
eventually you're not going to like them very much

408
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
and that frustration is going to

409
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
feed on itself until you conclude that they're not

410
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
able to do what you want them to do and

411
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
so you're going to start doing it instead

412
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
and

413
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
kind of all the energy drains out of

414
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
the program. and this happens all the time.

415
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
It's a very common thing to have happen,

416
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
and most of the time it happens because

417
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
the scouts involved are aimed at being able to

418
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
go to summer camp next summer

419
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
and say they have a boy led troop,

420
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
when what they really need to do

421
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
is aim at developing character.

422
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
understand that scouts are leading anyway.

423
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
observe their scouts

424
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
and start building, step by step,

425
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
upon the leadership skills that they already have.

426
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
and the foundational ideas

427
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
are very, very simple.

428
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
You'll spend a lifetime learning how to apply them,

429
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
but they're very, very simple.

430
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
They'll help you avoid situations like that.

431
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
So let's go back

432
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
and review them for a moment.

433
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
Remember your aim is not developing

434
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
leaders, is developing character

435
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
and young people through the patrol method

436
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
and constantly referencing the scout

437
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
oath and law. in that context,

438
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
Leadership is only a skill.

439
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
It's one of the many skills

440
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
required to make this whole process

441
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
happen. It's a means to an end

442
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
and not an end in itself.

443
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
Let's remember that every scout

444
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
comes to us with

445
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
some leadership capability in one

446
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
of four ways:

447
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
They're able to lead themselves,

448
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
they're able to follow cooperatively,

449
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
they're able to

450
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
help other people at all times

451
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
and then they're able to direct

452
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
and decide about

453
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
the activities for their troop and patrol.

454
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
So when we begin with that premise

455
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
and we start looking

456
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
for leadership, then we

457
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
have something we can develop as a skill.

458
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
Training leaders

459
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
is not the same as developing

460
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
leaders. Let's stop training them

461
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
and start developing them.

462
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
to develop leaders,

463
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
you balance direction versus coercion

464
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
and shared responsibility

465
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
versus autonomy. The concept

466
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
really is just that simple.

467
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
the practice,

468
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
oh. it's a lot more complicated

469
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
and it's great fun

470
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
and it's very worthy work to do,

471
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
because as you go through

472
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
this process aimed

473
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
at developing character, by

474
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
constantly referencing the ideals

475
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
in the scout oath and law,

476
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
by applying the patrol method,

477
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
you will see leadership skills

478
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
begin to develop. It's important to notice them,

479
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
it's important to nurture them

480
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
as they go along and have

481
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
a realistic expectation

482
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
and a way of responding to the skill

483
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
level of your scouts.

484
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
You won't make a skilled directive

485
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
leader overnight,

486
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
but it'll happen a lot quicker than you think.

487
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
and when scouts are

488
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
given the opportunity to lead-

489
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
remember if you want them to be leaders,

490
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
you got to let them lead.

491
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
When they're given that opportunity,

492
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
in spite of the mistakes they make

493
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
and the difficulties they will encounter,

494
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
you will be absolutely amazed

495
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
at what they are capable of doing

496
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
Well. thank you for listening

497
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
to this 367th edition

498
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
of the Scoutmaster

499
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
podcast. Love to have you get in touch

500
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
with me. It's not that difficult

501
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
to do. Go to

502
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
scoutmastercgcom

503
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
and use the contact form there.

504
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
